static.solution:
(3,3) in the  \vec u= (\boldsymbol{i}+\boldsymbol{j})/\sqrt{2} direction passes through (2,2) then (3,3) then (4,4).  One can use these points to estimate f_{\vec u}(3,3) in two ways,


 forward estimate: 
f_{\vec u}(3,3) \approx \frac{f(4,4)-f(3,3)}{\lvert(4,4)-(3,3)\rvert}
 backward estimate: 
f_{\vec u}(3,3) \approx \frac{f(3,3)-f(2,2)}{\lvert(3,3)-(2,2)\rvert}



Usually the average of these two is a better estimate than either one: 
\begin{aligned} \text{better estimate} \amp = \frac{1}{2}\left((\text{forward estimate})+(\text{backward estimate})\right) \\
\amp =\frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{2-5}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{5-6}{\sqrt{2}}\right) \\
\amp =-1.41421 \end{aligned}
